  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1208 PM MDT Tue Oct 27 2026\n\nCHILLY AND DRY WEEK:\n\nNear-zonal flow is established across the Intermountain West \ntoday, maintaining relatively dry conditions for our CWA. This \nkeeps precipitation chances near-zero for most locations with \nlittle more than scattered cloud cover expected. We're sitting \nnear-normal in terms of high temperatures as well, leaving \nvalleys in the 50's to low-60's, and mountain locations in the \n40's to low-50's. Though we continue to dry out steadily the \nnext few days, some valley locations may still experience fog \naround sunrise which may impact morning commutes.\n\nNEXT ROUND OF PRECIPITATION THIS WEEKEND:\n\nAs flow turns southwesterly ahead of a PacNW low, moisture \nbegins its return to the region. The first signs of this pattern\nshift are expected on Friday as isolated storms will begin to \npop up, though at this point QPF should be on the minimal side. \nBy Sunday though, moisture will be hitting its peak in our CWA. \nAs guidance stands there is little confidence in the exact \ntrajectory of the broad moisture plume. If we are to lean one \nway or another, more evidence is suggesting that the strongest \nportion of the plume will pass to our south, though even in this\nscenario, southwestern Colorado is in line for yet another wet \nstretch with PWAT's up to 300% of normal. Adding to the \ncomplexity of this event is the progression of the upper-level \npattern. As the PacNW low propagates eastwards towards the \nnorthern Rockies, a second low develops at the base of the \ntrough, swinging through the southwestern portion of the county \nbefore eventually rejoining the parent trough early in the work \nweek. The movement of both lows would likely have significant \nimpacts on the nature of this storm system as temperatures will \nplummet to around 10 degrees below normal with their passage. \nThis could be enough to drop snow level 1-2 kft. The complex \npattern continues to lead to weak confidence in the timing and \nlocation of any eventual snowfall though, and as such we cannot \nyet speculate on how impacts will manifest from said snowfall. \nWhat we can say now though is that any notable amount of \nprecipitation over southwestern Colorado locations recovering \nfrom last week's rainfall will have the potential to exacerbate \nflooding concerns. We are keeping a very close eye on this next \nround of precipitation as a result, and over the coming days as \nmore details come into view we will begin to better gauge the \nrisks Sunday into Monday morning. As such, stay tuned for \nadditional information throughout this week. &&",
